        result of running arrow_keys on the example typewriter.

 Instructions:
    compile: make
    run: ./typewriter textfile.txt logfile.txt

 Data structures
    For action stacks, stacks allowed for easy push and pop, and last
    in first out access for undoing and redoing actions. I implemented the
    text as a vector of strings in order to fit nicely with the
    provided UI function. I could also have implemented it
    as a binary search tree, but that would have required constant
    conversion to the vector for the render function. It would, however,
    have allowed quicker editing. In the current format, if I'm editing
    the first line of a long text, I have to copy the rest of the
    text over, which would be O(n) where n is the length/number of characters
    in the text. With a tree, editing the first line might be closer to log(n)
    because I wouldn't have to copy as much over.

    Arrow keys in wrapped lines, undo and redo are complex. For arrows in 
    wrapped lines, we must aim to match the display column as much as possible
    (which is the actual column mod the screen width). If even the display
    column is longer than the line being moved to, we must move to the end
    of that line.
    For undo and redo, characters of matching insertion/deletion status that
    are not new lines are continued to be undone or redone together, and new
    lines, whether inserted or deleted, are undone and redone one at a time,
    with no other actions.

 Detail
    Fow a while, I edited the provided files (threelonglines, mindfulness, and
    maggies farm). I started with arrow keys in the simplest situations, and
    then expanded to when they wrap. Then I added enter and backspace. Once I
    got to undo and redo, I realized that I had to refactor most of the other
    functions into two pieces: one that did the actual work, and one that 
    updated the cursor. For example, "insert new line" does most of the work
    of physically updating the text, and "enter" calls that and also updates
    the cursor. Separating these out allowed me to be able to call most of the
    functionality from undo and redo without separating out the cursor. I soon
    realized I hadn't worked with new files, and edited my filestream handling
    to be able to add new files. I found it challenging to keep track of the 
    cursor when undoing and redoing new line characters. I built keylogger
    files and ran them against both my typewriter and the class one. In 
    submission they were all ran on threelonglines but throughout testing
    process I tried them on other texts as well. These tests make sure to test
    arrow keys, undoing and redoing a line, undoing and redoing a character,
    backspace and enter, and simply writing a new line. 


 Hours
 I spent probably 10 hours on part one, and 25 hours on part two.